  {
    id:'opqb-t27-1', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'23-year-old teacher complains that her right eye is red and imitated. You note moderate injection of the larger conjunctival vessels, watery discharge, and a palpable preauricular lymph node. From this information alone, what tentative diagnosis would you make?',
    options:['Bacterial conjunctivitis','Vernal kerato-conjunctivitis','Viral conjunctivitis','Atopic allergic conjunctivitis'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'**Three findings are given and all three point the same way.** `L37) Red Eye` Table 4-2 is the discriminator table this question is built on, and the row that decides it is the last one: **preauricular lymph-node enlargement is `+` for viral conjunctivitis and `-` for every other column in the table** — acute glaucoma, acute iridocyclitis, keratitis, bacterial conjunctivitis and allergic conjunctivitis alike. The chapter says the same thing in prose: *"Enlargement of the lymph node just in front of the auricle is a frequent sign of viral conjunctivitis. Usually, such enlargement does not occur in acute bacterial conjunctivitis."*\n\n**The discharge confirms it.** The same chapter grades discharge by character: **purulent or mucopurulent suggests a bacterial cause; serous (watery, clear or yellow-tinged) suggests a viral cause**; scant white stringy discharge belongs to allergic disease and dry eye. The stem prints *watery*.\n\n**And the pattern of redness is right for a conjunctivitis rather than anything deeper.** The stem says **injection of the larger conjunctival vessels** — that is conjunctival hyperaemia, not the perilimbal ciliary flush of keratitis, iridocyclitis or acute glaucoma. Table 4-2 gives ciliary flush as `+` for those three and `-` for all three conjunctivitides, and Figure 4-12 makes the point that conjunctival dilation is *less* intense perilimbally, the opposite of ciliary flush.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Bacterial conjunctivitis** | ⚠️ The intended trap, and both given signs exclude it. Bacterial disease gives **purulent or mucopurulent** discharge, not watery, and the node is explicitly *not* enlarged. The chapter also notes bacterial conjunctivitis is relatively uncommon. |\n| **Vernal kerato-conjunctivitis** | An allergic disease whose cardinal symptom is **itching** (Table 4-1 scores itching 2–3 for allergic and 0 for everything else), and whose signs are **giant tarsal papillae** and limbal changes. It is also typically bilateral and seasonal in a younger patient — none of that is in the stem, and no node. |\n| **Atopic allergic conjunctivitis** | Same objection. Itching is the entry ticket to the allergic column, the discharge is scant and stringy rather than watery, and preauricular nodes are not a feature. |\n\n**⚠️ SOURCE DEFECT, not repaired:** the stem prints *"red and imitated"* where the sense is **irritated**; confirmed at 3x zoom off book-160.png. Left exactly as printed — see `source`.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Watery discharge + palpable preauricular node + normal vision = adenoviral conjunctivitis until proved otherwise, and the node is the single most useful bedside sign. The same sign is tested from the House book at `opmcq-c6-2` and `opmcq-c6-16`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Use watery discharge and a palpable preauricular node to identify viral conjunctivitis, and separate it from bacterial and allergic conjunctivitis on discharge character, itching and node status.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.160, Q1 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECT kept as printed: "red and imitated" for "irritated". Supporting: `L37) Red Eye` Table 4-2 (preauricular node + for viral only; ciliary flush absent in conjunctivitis), Table 4-1 (itching 2–3 only in allergic), and the prose on discharge character and preauricular node enlargement.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-2', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'In the previous Q, based on the above information, which of the following symptoms or facts might be elicited by careful history taking?',
    options:['Blurred vision','Sore throat','Exposure to children with colds','Itching'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'**The question is asking what else adenovirus does, and the chapter answers it by name.** `L37) Red Eye`, under *Associated Systemic Problems*, prints: *"Infection of the upper respiratory tract accompanied by fever may be associated with conjunctivitis, particularly when these symptoms are due to adenovirus type 3 or type 7 (both of which cause **pharyngoconjunctival fever**)."* Pharyngo- is the sore throat; conjunctival is the red eye; fever completes the triad. So in a patient already diagnosed as viral on Q1, a **sore throat** is the systemic symptom the history is meant to turn up.\n\n**Why the other three are not merely weaker but positively wrong for this patient:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Blurred vision** | Excluded by the diagnosis. Table 4-1 scores blurred vision **0** for all three conjunctivitides and 1–3 for glaucoma, iridocyclitis and keratitis, and the chapter’s Points to Remember states it outright: *"If visual acuity is acutely and significantly reduced, a diagnosis of conjunctivitis is extremely unlikely."* Eliciting blurred vision would overturn Q1, not support it. |\n| **Exposure to children with colds** | ⚠️ **The near-miss, and worth being honest about.** The same chapter does write that *"a history of a cold or contact with others who have similar symptoms suggests a viral conjunctivitis"*, so this is a real and elicitable fact. It is the weaker answer for two reasons: it is an **exposure**, not a *symptom* of this patient, and it is a non-specific risk factor rather than the named syndrome the material attaches to adenovirus. The bank keys **B**, and B is the answer the material names. Recorded here so the reasoning is visible rather than glossed. |\n| **Itching** | The allergic column’s cardinal symptom — Table 4-1 gives itching **2–3** for allergic conjunctivitis and **0** for viral. Eliciting itching would move the diagnosis away from viral, not towards it. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In a suspected adenoviral conjunctivitis, ask specifically about **sore throat, fever and recent upper-respiratory infection** — the pharyngoconjunctival fever triad — and about contacts, since the same chapter’s advice to the patient is to avoid communal activities while discharge is present.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Recall pharyngoconjunctival fever as the adenovirus type 3/7 syndrome and identify sore throat as the systemic symptom to elicit in a viral conjunctivitis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.160, Q2 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). This is a chained item — it depends on Q1 (`opqb-t27-1`) for its clinical picture. Supporting: `L37) Red Eye` (pharyngoconjunctival fever, adenovirus types 3 and 7; history of a cold or contact; Table 4-1 symptom grid; Points to Remember on acuity).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-3', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'In the previous Q, Management consists of which of the following?',
    options:['Topical mast cell stabilizer','Broad-spectrum antibiotic eyedrops','Referral to an ophthalmologist','Strict hygienic measure and cold compresses'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:'**Viral conjunctivitis has no drug, and the chapter says so in its first clause:** *"There is no specific medicinal treatment for viral conjunctivitis, although patients should be instructed in proper precautions to prevent contagion"* (`L37) Red Eye`). What follows is a four-item list, and option D is two of those items compressed into one line:\n\n- **Apply cool compresses periodically throughout the day.**\n- Use artificial tears if irritation occurs.\n- Apply antibiotic eyedrops **only if bacterial infection is suspected**.\n- **Minimize spread to other family members and co-workers** — *washing hands after touching eye secretions, no sharing of towels*.\n\nTable 4-3 of the same chapter, the patient-instruction table, repeats it as the advice to hand the patient: *"Apply cool compresses periodically. Use artificial tears if needed. Wash hands frequently and avoid touching eyes and sharing towels. Avoid communal activities as long as discharge is present. Return for referral if symptoms appear to worsen."*\n\n**Why hygiene is not an afterthought here.** Adenoviral conjunctivitis is the classically contagious red eye — the reason the chapter attaches communal-activity restriction to it and to no other conjunctivitis. In a **teacher**, as Q1 specifies, the hygiene half of option D is the part with public-health consequences.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Topical mast cell stabilizer** | Treats the **allergic** column — the one this patient was excluded from at Q1 on the absence of itching and the presence of a preauricular node. A mast-cell stabiliser does nothing to an adenovirus. |\n| **Broad-spectrum antibiotic eyedrops** | ⚠️ The commonest real-world error, and the chapter fences it precisely: antibiotic drops are for *"if bacterial infection is suspected"*. In a watery, node-positive picture bacterial infection is not suspected, so this is unnecessary antibiotic exposure. |\n| **Referral to an ophthalmologist** | Over-escalation. `L37) Red Eye` opens by classing conjunctivitis among the conditions that *"either resolve spontaneously or can be treated easily by the primary care provider"*, and Table 4-3 makes referral conditional — *"Return for referral if symptoms appear to worsen."* Nothing in Q1 (normal vision, no ciliary flush, no pupillary abnormality, no corneal opacity) triggers the referral criteria in Table 4-2. |\n\n**⚠️ SOURCE DEFECTS, not repaired:** the stem prints a mid-sentence capital in *"Q, Management consists"*, and option D prints *"measure"* singular. Both left as printed.\n\n**A caution the chapter attaches to this exact scenario:** *"Corticosteroids have limited use in treatment of infectious conjunctivitis"*, and combined antibiotic–steroid drops *"should be used only under the close observation of an ophthalmologist"* — steroids markedly potentiate herpes simplex and fungal keratitis, cause cataract on prolonged use, and raise IOP in about a third of the population after 2–6 weeks.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: op-red-mgmt.)*',
    objective:'Manage viral conjunctivitis with cool compresses and contagion precautions rather than antibiotics, mast-cell stabilisers or referral, and state the conditions under which each of those would instead be indicated.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.160, Q3 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECTS kept as printed: mid-sentence capital in "Q, Management consists"; option D "measure" singular. Chained to `opqb-t27-1`. Supporting: `L37) Red Eye` (viral conjunctivitis treatment list; Table 4-3 patient instructions; antibiotic drops only if bacterial suspected; corticosteroid warnings).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-4', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'65-year-old man with history of a recent bronchitis awoke this morning a red eye and has no other symptoms. He has no significant me problems. On examination, the patient has a sector of the eye that is solid red with injection of the conjunctival vessels. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
    options:['Scleritis','Subconjunctival hemorrhage','Early viral conjunctivitis','Pterygium'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'**Four words decide it: sectoral, solid red, painless, and bronchitis.**\n\n`L37) Red Eye` defines the lesion as *"an accumulation of blood in the potential space between the conjunctiva and the sclera"*, and gives its three negatives: it *"may look alarming"* but is *"rarely vision threatening unless associated with significant ocular trauma"*; *"the patient may note some ocular irritation but should not complain of significant pain"*; and *"vision should be unaffected."* The stem supplies all three — a man who **awoke** with it, **no other symptoms**, no pain and no visual complaint.\n\n**Bronchitis is the aetiology, not scenery.** The same chapter lists the causes as *"a sudden increase in ocular venous pressure, such as occurs with coughing, sneezing, vomiting, or vigorous rubbing of the eye"*. A recent bronchitis means paroxysmal coughing, which is the classic venous-pressure spike. The chapter adds that in the absence of blunt trauma the haemorrhage *"requires no treatment and, unless recurrent, no evaluation"*.\n\n**Why "a sector" matters.** Blood in a potential space spreads as a **flat, sharply edged, uniformly red patch** with normal white conjunctiva around it — you cannot see individual vessels through it. That is what "solid red" means, and it is the opposite of hyperaemia, which is *"a diffuse dilation of the conjunctival blood vessels"* (`L37) Red Eye`, Fig 4-12) in which individual vessels remain visible. `L6) Conjunctiva` teaches the appearance only by photograph — slide 35 shows *"several flat, bright-red, sharply-edged patches of SUBCONJUNCTIVAL HAEMORRHAGE lying on the otherwise whitish bulbar conjunctiva"* — and lists it as a titled slide of the deck.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Scleritis** | Wrong on pain above all. Scleritis is the severe, boring, night-waking ocular pain of the red-eye differential, typically with a violaceous rather than blood-red hue and with tenderness on palpation; the stem says explicitly *no other symptoms*. |\n| **Early viral conjunctivitis** | ⚠️ The trap, because Q1–Q3 have just drilled viral conjunctivitis. But conjunctivitis is **diffuse, not sectoral**, and comes with discharge and (per Table 4-2) a preauricular node. None is present. |\n| **Pterygium** | A **chronic degeneration**, not an overnight event — a fleshy, vascular, wing-shaped fold growing from the limbus onto the cornea over months to years, UV-driven. It does not appear on waking, and it is not solid red. |\n\n**⚠️ SOURCE DEFECTS, not repaired.** The stem prints *"no significant me problems"* where the sense is **eye**, and *"awoke this morning a red eye"* is missing a preposition (*with*). Both confirmed at 3x zoom. There is also an **internal contradiction in the stem worth naming**: *"solid red with injection of the conjunctival vessels"* asks for both signs at once, when solid red (extravasated blood) and vessel injection (dilated vessels) are the two mutually exclusive patterns Table 4-2 separates. The key is unaffected — everything else in the stem is haemorrhage — and the key is not disputed.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: op-red.)*',
    objective:'Diagnose subconjunctival haemorrhage from a painless sectoral solid-red eye with normal vision after a venous-pressure spike, and separate it from diffuse conjunctival hyperaemia.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.160, Q4 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECTS kept as printed: "no significant me problems" for "eye"; "awoke this morning a red eye" missing a preposition; and the stem asks for solid red and vessel injection together. Supporting: `L37) Red Eye` (subconjunctival haemorrhage definition, painlessness, normal vision, causes including coughing; Fig 4-12 on diffuse hyperaemia; Table 4-2); `L6) Conjunctiva` sl.35 and the deck title list.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-5', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'In the previous Q, Management would consist of which of the following?',
    options:['Oral NSAIDS','Assurance and follow up','Topical steroid','Urgent referral to ophthalmologist'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'**The chapter prescribes nothing, and that is the answer.** `L37) Red Eye`: *"In the absence of blunt trauma, hemorrhage into the subconjunctival space … requires no treatment and, unless recurrent, no evaluation."* Table 4-3, the patient-instruction table, gives the whole management as one sentence to say out loud: *"Know that without treatment the hemorrhage will resolve in 1 to 2 weeks without any damage to the eye."* Reassurance plus follow-up is exactly that sentence turned into an option.\n\n**Why follow-up is still part of the answer.** Two qualifiers in the chapter’s own wording keep this from being pure dismissal. *"Unless recurrent"* — repeated spontaneous haemorrhages are what prompt an evaluation (blood pressure, antiplatelet or anticoagulant use, a bleeding tendency), so the patient must be seen again to know whether it recurs. And *"in the absence of blunt trauma"* — this stem has none, but a haemorrhage after trauma is a different problem entirely, since the chapter’s own caveat is that it is rarely vision-threatening *"unless associated with significant ocular trauma"*. Follow-up is where both qualifiers get tested.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Oral NSAIDS** | Treating a symptom the patient does not have. The chapter is explicit that the patient *"should not complain of significant pain"*; the stem says *no other symptoms*. Worse, NSAIDs are antiplatelet — in an elderly man who has just bled subconjunctivally, they push in the wrong direction. *(The antiplatelet objection is __not taken from the course material__; the absence of pain is the chapter’s.)* |\n| **Topical steroid** | No inflammation to suppress — this is extravasated blood, not inflammatory hyperaemia — and the chapter’s therapeutic warnings list three serious harms from topical corticosteroids: potentiation of **herpes simplex and fungal keratitis**, **cataract** with prolonged use, and an IOP rise in about **one third** of the population after 2–6 weeks. It states plainly that they *"should not be administered to the eye by a primary care provider."* |\n| **Urgent referral to ophthalmologist** | The chapter files subconjunctival haemorrhage among the conditions that *"either resolve spontaneously or can be treated easily by the primary care provider"*. Table 4-2 marks it **"referral advisable"** for signs this patient does not have — corneal opacification or epithelial disruption, pupillary abnormality, shallow anterior chamber, raised IOP, proptosis — while conjunctival hyperaemia and discharge are marked "No". |\n\n**⚠️ SOURCE DEFECTS, not repaired:** mid-sentence capital in *"Q, Management would consist"*, and option A prints *"NSAIDS"* in full capitals with no apostrophe. Both as printed.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: op-red-mgmt.)*',
    objective:'Manage a non-traumatic subconjunctival haemorrhage by reassurance and follow-up, quoting the 1–2 week resolution, and justify withholding NSAIDs, steroids and urgent referral.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.160, Q5 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECTS kept as printed: mid-sentence capital in "Q, Management would consist"; option A "NSAIDS". Chained to `opqb-t27-4`. Supporting: `L37) Red Eye` (no treatment and no evaluation unless recurrent; Table 4-3 "resolve in 1 to 2 weeks"; Table 4-2 referral column; therapeutic warnings on topical corticosteroids).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-6', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'54-year-old woman with a red, painful eye and the patient is complaining of photophobia When you examine her, you note that visual acuity is decreased in the affected eye and cornea appears opaque and hazy with positive fluoresceine staining. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
    options:['Conjunctivitis','Corneal ulcer','Old corneal scar','Scleritis'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'**Fluorescein positivity is the whole question.** `L37) Red Eye` states the rule in its Points to Remember: *"Fluorescein should always be instilled in a red eye to test for integrity of the corneal epithelium."* A positive stain means the epithelium is **broken**. Table 4-2 gives **corneal epithelial disruption** as `+` for keratitis alone and `-` for acute glaucoma, iridocyclitis and bacterial and allergic conjunctivitis (`+/-` for viral). Corneal opacification is likewise `+` for keratitis. An epithelial defect with a hazy, opaque cornea **is** a corneal ulcer.\n\n**The other three findings fit and are worth naming individually.** `L37) Red Eye` links them: *"Disruption of the corneal epithelium results in significant pain for the patient"*, and *"loss of the integrity of the corneal epithelium accompanied with infection or inflammation can result in an ulcer with associated hyperemia. Often the normally clear cornea appears hazy or white in the area of the ulcer. Mucus secretions in the eye … and pain are common as well as photophobia."* The **decreased acuity** completes it — Table 4-1 scores blurred vision **3** for keratitis and **0** for every conjunctivitis, and the chapter states outright that if acuity is acutely and significantly reduced, *"a diagnosis of conjunctivitis is extremely unlikely."*\n\n**What the ulcer is likely to be.** `L15,16) Cornea` gives bacterial keratitis as *"pain, photophobia, blurred vision, mucopurulent discharge – acute onset"* with signs of *"epithelial defect with stromal infiltrate + circumcorneal injection"*, risk factors led by **contact lens wear** in the developed world and **ocular trauma, agricultural injury** in the developing world, and organisms **Staphylococcus aureus, S. pneumoniae, Streptococcus pyogenes, Pseudomonas aeruginosa**. The stem gives no lens history and no organism, so the diagnosis stops correctly at "corneal ulcer".\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Conjunctivitis** | Excluded twice over — the acuity is **decreased**, and the cornea **stains**. Table 4-1 gives conjunctivitis 0 for blurred vision, 0 for pain and 0 for photophobia. |\n| **Old corneal scar** | An opacity, yes — but an **inactive** one. A scar is painless, quiet, does not reduce a white eye to redness, and above all **does not take up fluorescein**, because its epithelium is intact. The word "old" is doing the trapping. |\n| **Scleritis** | Deep, boring, night-waking pain with a violaceous scleral hue and tenderness on palpation, but with an **intact epithelium** — no fluorescein uptake and no corneal haze. It sits in the red-eye differential for pain, not for staining. |\n\n**⚠️ SOURCE DEFECTS, not repaired:** there is no full stop after *"photophobia"*, so two sentences run together at *"photophobia When"*, and the stem prints *"fluoresceine"* for **fluorescein**. Both confirmed at 3x zoom; both left as printed.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Red eye + pain + photophobia + reduced acuity + fluorescein uptake = corneal ulcer, and that combination is a referral, not a prescription. The management half is asked immediately afterwards at `opqb-t27-7`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: op-red.)*',
    objective:'Diagnose a corneal ulcer from fluorescein-positive corneal haze with pain, photophobia and reduced acuity, and use epithelial integrity to exclude conjunctivitis, an old scar and scleritis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.160, Q6 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECTS kept as printed: missing full stop at "photophobia When"; "fluoresceine" for fluorescein. Supporting: `L37) Red Eye` (Tables 4-1 and 4-2; epithelial disruption and pain; ulcer appearance; Points to Remember on fluorescein and on acuity); `L15,16) Cornea` (bacterial keratitis features, risk factors and organisms).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-7', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'In the previous Q, regarding your management, which of the following should be avoided?',
    options:['Intensive topical antimicrobial eye drops','Topical cycloplegic eye drops','Scraping for culture and sensitivity','Topical steroid therapy'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:'**The cornea deck prints this as an instruction, not a nuance:** under *Bacterial Keratitis: Treatment*, `L15,16) Cornea` lists **"AVOID topical steroids until infectious cause is excluded or controlled"** — the last line of the treatment block, sitting directly beneath the three interventions that make up the other three options.\n\n**Why steroids are dangerous in an infected cornea.** `L37) Red Eye` gives the mechanism among its three therapeutic warnings: *"Both herpes simplex keratitis … and fungal keratitis are markedly potentiated by corticosteroids. Corticosteroids may mask symptoms of inflammation, making the patient ‘feel’ better, while the cornea may be melting away or even perforating."* The cornea deck makes the same point at maximum strength for the herpetic case: **"NEVER use topical steroids in EPITHELIAL herpes keratitis – this promotes viral replication and can result in corneal perforation."** Since the organism in Q6 is unknown, a steroid risks exactly the two infections it most accelerates.\n\n**Why the other three are the correct management, not distractors to be dismissed:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Intensive topical antimicrobial eye drops** | The **first-line treatment**. `L15,16) Cornea`: *"First-line: intensive topical fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin or ofloxacin) – hourly day 1, then taper"*, with new-generation fluoroquinolones (moxifloxacin, gatifloxacin) for resistant or Gram-positive organisms. |\n| **Topical cycloplegic eye drops** | Also correct, and the deck gives both reasons: *"Cycloplegic drops: reduce pain from ciliary spasm, prevent synechiae."* |\n| **Scraping for culture and sensitivity** | Correct, and time-critical. The deck’s clinical pearl reads: *"Corneal scraping should be performed in most cases BEFORE starting antibiotics so that the causative organism can be cultured and its sensitivity determined"*, and its investigation protocol ends **"ALWAYS scrape before starting antibiotics"** — scalpel No.11 or 21G needle, Gram stain, culture on blood, chocolate and Sabouraud agar. |\n\n**The one place steroids *are* used, so the rule is not learned as a blanket ban.** `L15,16) Cornea` permits them in **disciform (stromal immune) keratitis** — *"Treat with TOPICAL STEROIDS + antivirals"* — and in **necrotizing stromal keratitis** as a *"combined antiviral + steroid (careful balance)"*. The discriminator is immune-mediated stromal disease with an intact epithelium versus active epithelial infection, and even then the deck adds *"always combined with antiviral cover"* and *"only use … under ophthalmologist"*.\n\n**Why the whole question matters at primary-care level.** `L37) Red Eye` closes its steroid warning with the line that decides who may prescribe: topical corticosteroids alone or with an antibiotic *"should not be administered to the eye by a primary care provider."* Prolonged use additionally causes **cataract**, and 2–6 weeks of local application raises IOP in about **one third** of the population, with possible optic nerve damage.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Identify topical steroids as the intervention to avoid in an undiagnosed corneal ulcer, and justify intensive topical antimicrobials, cycloplegia and pre-antibiotic scraping as the correct management.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.161, Q7 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). Chained to `opqb-t27-6`. Supporting: `L15,16) Cornea` (bacterial keratitis treatment block including "AVOID topical steroids until infectious cause is excluded or controlled"; scraping pearl and investigation protocol; disciform and necrotizing exceptions; the epithelial-HSV steroid prohibition); `L37) Red Eye` (therapeutic warnings on topical corticosteroids).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-8', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'45-year-old man reports a 2-day history of redness, pain, and photophobia of his left eye. He denies any trauma to the eye, fluoresceine staining reveals dendritic corneal ulcer. Management would consist of which of the following?',
    options:['Topical antiviral like acyclovir','Topical steroid therapy','Topical antibiotics','Assurance and follow-up in one week'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:'**The cornea deck answers this question in a worked scenario that is almost word for word the stem.** `L15,16) Cornea`, *Clinical Scenario 2: The Dendritic Ulcer* — *"A 30-year-old presents with a 3-day history of mild eye pain, photophobia, and redness. Slit lamp with fluorescein shows a linear branching lesion with terminal buds in the central cornea. What should you do?"* — and its printed answer is: **"Topical antiviral (aciclovir 3% ointment 5x/day or ganciclovir gel) – NO TOPICAL STEROIDS."** Diagnosis: **herpes simplex epithelial (dendritic) keratitis**.\n\n**Why the dendrite is diagnostic on its own.** The deck files epithelial HSV as *"active virus replication in epithelium"* with the *"classic dendritic ulcer with terminal buds"*, and stains it two ways: **fluorescein stains the ulcer bed, Rose Bengal stains the virus-laden margins**. It calls the **terminal buds on the branches pathognomonic of the HSV dendrite** — and distinguishes the herpes zoster **pseudodendrite**, which is *"smaller, stellate, lacks terminal buds"*. HSV is *"the commonest infectious cause of corneal disease in developed countries"*, which is why no trauma and no contact lens history is needed to reach it.\n\n**Two adjuncts the deck adds, worth knowing beyond the key:** *"Debridement of infected epithelium (swab) can be adjunctive"*, and for recurrent disease *"prophylactic oral aciclovir 400 mg twice daily reduces recurrence rate by ~50%"*. **⚠️ That dose is quoted from the lecture text as printed; doses are not read from OCR in this project.**\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Topical steroid therapy** | ⚠️ The dangerous answer, and the deck prints its prohibition in capitals: **"NEVER use topical steroids in EPITHELIAL herpes keratitis – this promotes viral replication and can result in corneal perforation."** The scenario repeats it: *"Do NOT use steroids at this stage → risk of geographic ulcer → perforation."* Steroids belong to **disciform (stromal immune)** keratitis, always with antiviral cover — a different stage of the same disease. |\n| **Topical antibiotics** | Wrong pathogen. Antibacterials are the first-line for **bacterial** keratitis (intensive topical fluoroquinolones); they have no activity against HSV. The bacterial picture the deck contrasts is *rapid onset, mucopurulent discharge, large central or paracentral ulcer, hypopyon, contact lens history* — none of which is in the stem. |\n| **Assurance and follow-up in one week** | Treats an active, replicating epithelial infection by waiting. Untreated, the dendrite enlarges into a **geographic ulcer**; the necrotizing stromal form the deck describes is *"progressive stromal necrosis and thinning … may lead to perforation … very difficult to treat"*. A week is exactly the wrong interval. |\n\n**⚠️ SOURCE DEFECTS, not repaired:** the stem prints *"fluoresceine"* for **fluorescein**, and there is a comma splice at *"trauma to the eye, fluoresceine staining reveals"*. Left as printed.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Treat an epithelial (dendritic) herpes simplex keratitis with a topical antiviral and state explicitly why topical steroids are contraindicated at the epithelial stage.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.161, Q8 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECTS kept as printed: "fluoresceine" for fluorescein; comma splice at "trauma to the eye, fluoresceine staining reveals". Supporting: `L15,16) Cornea` (Clinical Scenario 2 with its printed answer; HSV epithelial features, terminal buds, fluorescein vs Rose Bengal; the epithelial-steroid prohibition; disciform exception; debridement and prophylactic aciclovir; bacterial-vs-HSV contrast table).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-9', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'50 years old female patient came to her family doctor complaining of redness of her left eye. Examination showed 28 mmHg IOP 6/24 VA, hazy cornea and pupillary constriction The most probable diagnosis is?',
    options:['Bacterial conjunctivitis','Acute closed angle glaucoma','Acute iridocyclitis','Bacterial keratitis'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'**One word settles this question, and it is the pupil.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 17 gives the pupil in anterior uveitis as **"Miosis with sluggish pupillary light reaction"**, with an irregular shape once synechiae form. `L12) Glaucoma 2` gives the pupil in acute angle closure as **"Mid-dilated vertically oval non-reactive pupil"**. The stem prints **pupillary constriction**. That is the uveitis pupil, not the glaucoma pupil, and it is the only sign in the stem that separates the two.\n\n**The rest of the picture is consistent with anterior uveitis throughout.** Slides 13–17 of the uvea deck list the symptoms as *usually unilateral, ocular pain, red eye, photophobia, blurred vision*, and the signs as *decreased vision, ciliary injection, keratic precipitates on the back surface of the cornea, anterior-chamber cells and flare ± hypopyon*, the miotic sluggish pupil — and, critically for this stem, **"IOP: usually decreased but may be normal or increased."** So **28 mmHg is compatible with iridocyclitis**; the deck explicitly allows a raised pressure. VA 6/24 matches *decreased vision*, and a hazy cornea is what keratic precipitates and anterior-chamber activity produce at this level of examination.\n\n**⚠️ The numerals were read off the page image at 3x zoom with their units: 28 mmHg IOP and 6/24 VA.** They are the reason the question is answerable — see the glaucoma column below.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Acute closed angle glaucoma** | ⚠️ The intended trap, and **28 mmHg is what defeats it**. `L12) Glaucoma 2` describes acute angle closure as a *"rapid severe rise of IOP"*, an **ocular emergency** with **"IOP → ++++ (stony hard)"** — a pressure of 50–80 mmHg, not 28. It also requires the **mid-dilated non-reactive pupil**, a **shallow anterior chamber or iris bombé**, and gives the symptoms as *pain ++++, coloured haloes, rapid diminution of vision over several hours, redness, nausea and vomiting*. The stem prints a constricted pupil and none of that symptom cluster. |\n| **Bacterial conjunctivitis** | Excluded by the acuity alone. `L37) Red Eye` Table 4-1 scores blurred vision **0** for bacterial conjunctivitis, and the chapter states that if acuity is acutely and significantly reduced, *"a diagnosis of conjunctivitis is extremely unlikely."* Conjunctivitis also does not raise IOP, cloud the cornea or alter the pupil (Table 4-2 marks pupillary abnormalities `-` for it). |\n| **Bacterial keratitis** | The cornea is hazy, but the stem reports **no epithelial defect and no fluorescein uptake** — and `L15,16) Cornea` builds bacterial keratitis on exactly that sign: *"epithelial defect with stromal infiltrate + circumcorneal injection"*, with mucopurulent discharge, a large central or paracentral ulcer, hypopyon and a contact-lens history. None is present, and keratitis does not cause a miotic pupil. |\n\n**⚠️ SOURCE DEFECTS, not repaired:** there is no full stop after *"constriction"*, so two sentences run together at *"constriction The"*, and the closing sentence is a **statement written with a question mark** (*"The most probable diagnosis is?"*). Both left as printed.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In a red eye with reduced vision and a raised-but-not-stony pressure, read the pupil: **small and sluggish ⇒ iridocyclitis; mid-dilated and fixed ⇒ angle closure.** The sequelae the uvea deck attaches to untreated anterior uveitis are **posterior synechiae** and **secondary glaucoma**, which is how a uveitis eventually earns a high pressure of its own.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: op-red.)*',
    objective:'Use pupil size and reaction, plus the magnitude of the IOP rise, to separate acute iridocyclitis from acute angle-closure glaucoma in a red eye with reduced vision.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.161, Q9 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). Numerals read off the page image at 3x zoom with their units: 28 mmHg IOP, 6/24 VA. ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECTS kept as printed: missing full stop at "constriction The"; a statement closing with a question mark. Supporting: `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` sl.13–18 (anterior uveitis symptoms and signs; miosis with sluggish reaction; IOP usually decreased but may be normal or increased; synechiae and secondary glaucoma); `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` (acute angle closure symptoms and signs — stony hard IOP, mid-dilated vertically oval non-reactive pupil, shallow AC/iris bombé); `L37) Red Eye` Tables 4-1 and 4-2; `L15,16) Cornea` (bacterial keratitis signs).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-10', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'60-year-old man notes sudden profound loss of vision in his right eye, with onset one hour ago, the patient has a past medical history of hypertension. On examination, the visual acuity is OD hand motion and OS 6/6 the pupil of the right eye shows RAPD The direct ophthalmoscope shows that the red reflex is clear in both eyes. Fundus examination in the right eyes reveals a white opacified retina with cherry red spot in the macular are. The left eye is normal. No other clinical manifestation What is your diagnosis?',
    options:['AION','B CRAO','Optic neuritis','Retinal detachment'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'**The fundus picture is printed almost word for word in the material.** `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` gives central retinal artery occlusion as: **VA < 6/60 · APD marked · retinal whitening · "cherry-red spot" at macula · arteriolar and venular narrowing · sludging and segmentation of the blood column (cattle-trucking) · very poor prognosis.** The stem supplies four of those six — hand-motion acuity, RAPD, a white opacified retina and a cherry-red spot at the macula — and adds sudden onset one hour ago in a hypertensive 60-year-old.\n\n**The clear red reflex is not a throwaway line.** It tells you the media are clear, so the acuity loss is retinal or neural, not a vitreous haemorrhage or a dense cataract. This is the observation that makes a *direct ophthalmoscope* enough to answer the question.\n\n**Where the deck files it.** `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` opens with a classification slide whose entire content is *"1. Painful / 2. Painless"*, and works CRAO as its Case 1 — history, findings, then a diagnosis slide reading **"Diagnosis / Central Retinal Artery Occlusion"**. Painless, sudden, unilateral, profound.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **AION** | Anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy also gives sudden painless monocular loss with an RAPD, so the acuity and the pupil do not separate them — **the fundus does**. AION swells the **disc**; the retina stays normal. There is no retinal whitening and no cherry-red spot, because the retinal circulation is intact. `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` teaches AION as its own case (Case 3) with headache, fatigue and weight loss, none of which the stem offers. |\n| **Optic neuritis** | Wrong age and wrong fundus. The deck’s papillitis case is a young patient, and neither papillitis nor the retrobulbar form whitens the retina or produces a cherry-red spot. Optic neuritis also typically carries **pain on eye movement** and a slower onset over hours to days; the stem gives one hour, painless, with a retinal lesion. |\n| **Retinal detachment** | The wrong red reflex and the wrong tempo. A detachment gives a **grey elevated retina** and a **greyish red reflex**, usually preceded by floaters, flashes and a progressing field defect — the deck works exactly that case (RRD) as Case 4. Here the red reflex is explicitly *clear in both eyes*, and the retina is flat and white, not elevated. |\n\n**⚠️ FOUR SOURCE DEFECTS, none repaired, all confirmed at 3x zoom.** (1) **Option B prints its own label twice** — the line reads *"B. B CRAO"*, so the option text is literally **"B CRAO"**; recorded verbatim, and the key is unaffected because the option remains second in the menu. (2) *"macular are."* for **macular area**. (3) Missing full stops after *"OS 6/6"*, after *"RAPD"* and after *"manifestation"*, each running two sentences together. (4) *"right eyes"* plural for one eye.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** White retina + cherry-red spot + RAPD + clear media = CRAO, and the clock started an hour ago. The management is asked next at `opqb-t27-11`. The same fundus is tested from the House book at `opmcq-c13-10` and `opmcq-c13-11`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: op-acute.)*',
    objective:'Diagnose central retinal artery occlusion from sudden painless monocular loss with an RAPD, a white opacified retina and a macular cherry-red spot, and separate it from AION, optic neuritis and retinal detachment on the fundus appearance.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.161, Q10 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️ FOUR SOURCE DEFECTS kept as printed: option B prints its label twice ("B. B CRAO"); "macular are." for "macular area"; missing full stops after "OS 6/6", "RAPD" and "manifestation"; "right eyes" plural. Supporting: `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` (CRAO sign list — VA < 6/60, marked APD, retinal whitening, cherry-red spot, arteriolar and venular narrowing, cattle-trucking, very poor prognosis); `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` (painful/painless classification sl.2; Case 1 diagnosis slide "Central Retinal Artery Occlusion"; the AION and RRD cases).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-11', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'In the previous Q, regarding your diagnosis, which of the following would be the most appropriate way of management?',
    options:['Systemic steroid','AC paracentesis','Carotid doppler U/S','Anti-VEGF'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'**⚠️ This is a genuine gap in the lecture material, and it is filled here rather than declared.** `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` heads a slide **"Management"** after its CRAO case and then prints **nothing on it** — one word on a bare white slide, no bullet, no drug, no time window. The file’s own not-covered register names the missing items explicitly: *"CRAO: ocular massage, **anterior chamber paracentesis**, IOP-lowering drops or acetazolamide, rebreathing/carbogen, the ‘retina survives ~90 minutes’ style time window, thrombolysis, and the stroke/carotid/cardiac work-up and referral. No time target of any kind is printed."* `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` gives the CRAO signs and the carotid imaging modalities but no acute treatment either.\n\n**The other bank does print it, so it is preferred to outside knowledge here.** `opmcq-c13-11` asks the same acute-management question from the House book and states the principle: nothing dissolves a retinal embolus acutely, so treatment attacks the other side of the perfusion equation — **lower the intraocular pressure fast, by IV mannitol, acetazolamide, ocular massage or anterior chamber paracentesis, to widen the arterial-to-intraocular pressure gradient and drive the embolus distally into a smaller branch**, sparing whatever retina lies proximal to it. AC paracentesis is the most abrupt of those, dropping the IOP within seconds.\n\n**Why urgency, not elegance, decides the answer.** The retina is end-arterial tissue and infarcts within a few hours of complete occlusion; the stem specifies **one hour ago**, which is the only window in which any of this is worth doing. *(The infarction time-course and the end-artery anatomy are __not taken from the course material__ — `L31,32` prints no time target at all and the retinal blood supply is in its not-covered register.)*\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Systemic steroid** | Belongs to the **arteritic** disease that CRAO’s neighbour AION can represent — giant cell arteritis — where steroid protects the fellow eye. It does nothing to an embolus, and `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` never prints the arteritic/non-arteritic split at all. Wrong disease, and no basis in this deck. |\n| **Carotid doppler U/S** | ⚠️ **Necessary but not acute — this is the trap.** `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` prints the carotid imaging block in full — **duplex scanning** (high-resolution real-time B-scan ultrasonography with Doppler flow analysis), digital intravenous subtraction angiography, magnetic resonance angiography, intra-arterial angiography — so the investigation is squarely in the material. But it is the **source hunt**, done after the eye has been treated, to prevent the next embolus and the stroke; it restores no vision today. The question asks for management one hour into the event. |\n| **Anti-VEGF** | Treats **neovascular and exudative** disease — diabetic macular oedema, wet AMD, and the neovascular complications of vein occlusion. There is no new vessel and no oedema in an acute arterial occlusion; anti-VEGF has no role at presentation. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Acute CRAO is treated by dropping the intraocular pressure immediately and investigating the carotid afterwards. Both halves are examinable; only one of them is the answer to *"most appropriate way of management"* an hour in.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: op-acute.)*',
    objective:'Select immediate IOP-lowering (anterior chamber paracentesis) as the acute management of CRAO, and place carotid duplex imaging as the subsequent source investigation rather than the acute treatment.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.161, Q11 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). Chained to `opqb-t27-10`. ⚠️ GAP FILLED: `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` prints a blank "Management" slide for CRAO and its not-covered register names anterior chamber paracentesis, ocular massage, IOP-lowering drops and the time window as absent; the answer is taken from the other bank (`opmcq-c13-11`, House), with the retinal infarction time-course tagged as not from the course material. Supporting: `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` (CRAO signs; carotid duplex, DIVSA, MRA and intra-arterial angiography).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-12', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'A healthy 48-year-old man complains of seeing" floating black dots in the field of vision of his right eyes for 2 days, associated with sensation of flashing light in the periphery of the visual field later on, he noticed a disturbance in the temporal field of vision of his right eye like "curtain coming down". His visual acuity is 6/6 in both eyes. Anterior segment examination is normal. The red reflex of the left eye is normal but in the right eye reveals area of grayish reflex What is your diagnosis?',
    options:['Vitreous hemorrhage','BRAO','Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment','Tractional retinal detachment'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'**The three symptoms arrive in the order the deck prints them, and that order is the diagnosis.** `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 11 gives rhegmatogenous detachment the symptom triad "**Floaters**, flashes of light, and a \'curtain\' or shadow in the peripheral vision", with progression "often sudden and rapid" — and the stem walks through those three in that same sequence: floating black dots for two days, flashing light in the periphery later on, then a temporal curtain coming down. Slide 12 repeats the same three labels on its RRD figure (Flashes of light · Dark floaters · Curtain) and draws the detached quadrant **pale grey against the orange attached retina** — which is precisely the "area of grayish reflex" found in the right eye and absent in the left. Slide 6 supplies the mechanism: a physical TEAR OR BREAK through which vitreous fluid seeps into the subretinal space, pushing the retina off its blood supply; it also calls RRD the MOST COMMON type, with ageing, high myopia and trauma as the causes. Slide 9 adds the surface sign — corrugated/wrinkled for rhegmatogenous, against taut for tractional and bullous for exudative.\n\nVisual acuity is still 6/6, so the fovea has not detached; the elevation is confined to the periphery, which is consistent with a curtain occupying only part of the temporal field. (That a temporal field defect corresponds to a nasal retinal detachment, because the retinal image is inverted, is *not taken from the course material* — the decks never print the mapping.)\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| A Vitreous hemorrhage | Floaters fit, but flashes followed by an advancing curtain do not, and blood in the vitreous dulls or extinguishes the **whole** red reflex rather than leaving one discrete grey patch beside otherwise normal reflex; acuity would rarely stay at 6/6. Vitreous haemorrhage gets no slide anywhere — `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` names it explicitly among the causes of sudden painless loss that receive no teaching — so it is excluded on general grounds, *not taken from the course material*. |\n| B BRAO | `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` prints branch retinal artery occlusion as variable VA, APD mild or absent, retinal whitening and arteriolar narrowing. That is an abrupt sectoral loss, complete from the first instant: no two-day prodrome of floaters, no photopsia, and the affected retina is **white**, not grey. |\n| **C Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment** | ✔ The printed key, and the deck\'s own triad, its own grey-quadrant reflex and its own most-common label. |\n| D Tractional retinal detachment | Slide 7: scar tissue contracts and lifts the retina, and "UNLIKE RRD, THERE IS TYPICALLY NO INITIAL TEAR"; the commonest cause is advanced diabetic retinopathy. Slide 11 gives TRD gradual vision loss with **distorted** or wavy vision, slow or progressive. This man is described as healthy and his history is two days old, and slide 9 gives tractional detachment a smooth/taut surface rather than a corrugated one. |\n\nCompare `opmcq-c13-4` (flashes and floaters with peripheral field loss) and `opmcq-c13-9` (causes of rhegmatogenous detachment) in the House bank.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: op-acute.)*',
    objective:'Recognise the floaters-flashes-curtain sequence with a localised grey red reflex as rhegmatogenous retinal detachment, and separate it from vitreous haemorrhage, branch arterial occlusion and tractional detachment on symptom tempo and fundus appearance.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.162, Q12 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECTS kept exactly as printed: an UNPAIRED straight double quote after "seeing" (the opening mark of the quotation is missing, confirmed at 3x zoom, while the later "curtain coming down" is properly paired), "his right eyes" in the plural, and no full stop after "grayish reflex". Supporting: `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slides 6, 7, 9, 11 and 12; `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` (BRAO); `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` (vitreous haemorrhage listed as untaught).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-13', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'34-year-old woman notes acute vision loss in her left eye. She is in otherwise good health. On examination, her visual acuity is OD 6/6, OS 6/60. defective color vision is noticed in her left eye. Examination of the pupillary light reflex reveals RAPD in the left eye. The anterior segment examination is normal. The red reflex is clear and fundus examination is normal in both eyes. What is your diagnosis?',
    options:['AION','Optic atrophy','Retrobulbar optic neuritis','Papillitis'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'**All four options are optic neuropathies, so the fundus is the entire discriminator — and here it is normal.** The vignette gives the complete optic nerve syndrome: unilateral acute loss to 6/60, defective colour vision, and an RAPD on the affected side. `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 8, the deck\'s one comparative table, assigns exactly that column to optic neuritis — Laterality unilateral, VA ↓, Colour V ↓, Contrast ↓, RAPD marked (the lecturer writes three ticks), Field central scotoma — against papilloedema, which keeps normal acuity, normal colour vision, no RAPD and gives only transient obscurations of seconds. What the table cannot tell you is **where along the nerve** the inflammation lies, and that is what this question is really asking. The disc looks normal, so the lesion is behind the globe: retrobulbar. Slide 4 names that form once, as "Chronic RB neuritis (centrocecal scotoma)", and the transcription records that the deck never expands "RB" and never states the normal-disc rule; that a retrobulbar lesion leaves an ophthalmoscopically normal disc — the old aphorism that the patient sees nothing and the doctor sees nothing — is *not taken from the course material*. Slide 8 does give the aetiologies of the inflammatory column as MS, viral, toxic and nutritional, which places a healthy 34-year-old woman with acute unilateral optic neuritis in the demyelinating group.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| A AION | Slide 9 shows AION as a swollen, PALE, chalky disc with blurred poles and a flame haemorrhage, evolving into a pale disc, with a superior altitudinal field defect on the deck\'s only perimetry printout, and its unlabelled clinical photograph is a thickened beaded temporal artery in an elderly patient. The disc is never normal in acute AION, and the age is wrong. |\n| B Optic atrophy | Slide 14 defines the four types purely by disc appearance — chalky white with sharp margins (primary), dirty grey-white with blurred margins (secondary), waxy yellow with attenuated vessels (consecutive), and pallor confined to a deep cup (glaucomatous). A normal-looking disc excludes every one of them, and atrophy is an end-state, not an acute event. |\n| **C Retrobulbar optic neuritis** | ✔ The printed key: the optic neuritis profile of slide 8 with a normal fundus. |\n| D Papillitis | Slide 7 photographs papillitis as a hyperaemic pink-red disc with blurred indistinct margins and no visible cup, the whole image veiled by the vitreous inflammatory cells slide 8 gives that column. The stem says the fundus is normal in both eyes, so the disc is not swollen and this cannot be papillitis. |\n\nCompare `opmcq-c14-12` (causes of chronic retrobulbar neuritis) and `opmcq-c14-21` (papillitis versus papilloedema) in the House bank.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: op-acute.)*',
    objective:'Use a normal optic disc to place an acute unilateral optic neuropathy with dyschromatopsia and an RAPD behind the globe, distinguishing retrobulbar neuritis from papillitis, AION and optic atrophy.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.162, Q13 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECT kept as printed: the sentence after "OS 6/60." begins with a lowercase "defective". Acuities read off the page image at 3x zoom — OD 6/6, OS 6/60. Supporting: `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 4 (field defects, "Chronic RB neuritis"), slide 7 (papillitis fundus), slide 8 (papilloedema/papillitis table), slide 9 (AION), slide 14 (the four optic atrophies).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-14', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:'43 years old patient complaining of blurred vision. On detailed history the vision problem is mainly during doing his work on laptop. Visual acuity is 6/6 in both eyes with normal IOP. Examination of the anterior segment reveals nuclear sclerosis, while fundus examination is normal in both eyes. What do you recommend for this patient?',
    options:['Glasses for distant vision correction','Preparing patient for phacoemulsification for nuclear sclerosis','Glasses for near vision correction','Assurance of the patient and scheduling follow up visits'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'**Forty-three years old, blurred only at the laptop, 6/6 at distance: this is presbyopia, and the nuclear sclerosis is the distractor.** `L7,8) Refractive errors` slide 73 defines presbyopia as recession of the near point and calls it "a normal aging process (usually in persons 40 years of age or older)"; slide 72 gives the amplitude of accommodation as roughly 14 D at 10 years, 3 D at 40, and effectively lost after 60. The failing task in this stem is near work alone — distance acuity is 6/6 in both eyes, the IOP is normal and the fundus is normal. Slide 81 is the deck\'s only treatment slide for presbyopia, and it prints correction with glasses: a spherical plus lens addition, dispensed as reading, bifocal or progressive spectacles. Option C is that slide.\n\nThe cataract deck disarms the nuclear sclerosis directly. `L18) Cataract II` gives nuclear cataract an increased nuclear refractive index producing acquired myopia, states that it "Interferes predominantly with **distant** vision", and prints "second sight" — the elderly temporarily *regaining* near vision as the lens turns myopic. A nuclear change therefore pushes near vision the wrong way to explain this complaint, and in this patient it has not cost a single line of distance acuity.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| A Glasses for distant vision correction | Distance acuity is already 6/6 in both eyes; there is no distance ametropia to correct. |\n| B Phacoemulsification for the nuclear sclerosis | `L18) Cataract II` heads its indications slide "Not all cataracts need operating" and lists the elective ones as visual impairment affecting quality of life, functional disability despite a glasses update, VA below the driving standard (typically below 6/12), monocular diplopia, refractive lens exchange, or facilitating posterior segment management. At 6/6 with a normal fundus he satisfies none of them, and none of the urgent indications (phacomorphic, phacolytic, phacoanaphylactic, dense congenital, subluxation with pupillary block) is present either. |\n| **C Glasses for near vision correction** | ✔ The printed key, and slide 81 verbatim: a plus addition for near. |\n| D Assurance of the patient and scheduling follow up visits | Half right and therefore the strongest wrong answer. Slide 73 does call presbyopia a normal ageing process, so reassurance belongs in the consultation — but slide 81 prints a treatment, the question asks what you recommend, and sending a symptomatic presbyope away with follow-up alone withholds the one intervention that relieves him. |\n\n⚠️ The deck prints no add power for any age — slide 81 gives the principle only — so no dioptric value is quoted here.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: op-cat.)*',
    objective:'Diagnose presbyopia in a patient over 40 whose blur is confined to near work with 6/6 distance acuity, and reject cataract surgery for an incidental nuclear sclerosis that fails every printed surgical indication.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.162, Q14 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECT kept as printed: "mainly during doing his work on laptop" — non-idiomatic, with no article before "laptop". Supporting: `L7,8) Refractive errors` slides 72, 73 and 81 (amplitude of accommodation, definition, the only treatment slide); `L18) Cataract II` (nuclear cataract, myopic shift, "second sight"; Indications for Cataract Surgery).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-15', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
    stem:'76-year-old man has noted visual distortion over the past week. His concern increased when he discovered that the distortion was in the right eye only. Straight lines viewed through his left eye remained straight. Visual acuity testing revealed OD 6/24, OS 6/6. What further tests will help determine the source of the patient\'s vision loss?',
    options:['Color vision test','Amsler grid test','Visual field test','OCT for optic'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'**Straight lines that bend is metamorphopsia, metamorphopsia localises to the macula, and the Amsler grid is the macular chart.** `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` prints the macular symptom list in full — diminution of vision, colour vision defects, micropsia, macropsia, metamorphopsia, scotoma — and the same deck makes age-related macular degeneration the leading cause of irreversible central visual loss "among people aged 52 or older", noting that patients with drusen alone "tend to have normal or near normal visual acuity, with minimal metamorphopsia". This man is 76, his distortion is strictly monocular, and the affected eye has already dropped to 6/24 against 6/6 in the fellow eye — distortion plus lost central acuity in one eye, which is the macular pattern rather than the drusen-only one. `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` gives the Amsler grid exactly one line, slide 123: "For the central 10° of the visual field". Slide 84 of the same deck draws metamorphopsia as a grid of dots with the middle rows bowed out of line — the deck\'s own picture of the symptom is the very chart being asked for.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| A Color vision test | Not absurd, because colour vision defects are on the same macular symptom list — but a colour test neither maps the distortion nor says where it comes from. In this course colour testing belongs to the optic nerve: `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 8 uses ↓ colour vision to separate papillitis from papilloedema. |\n| **B Amsler grid test** | ✔ The printed key. The one instrument the material assigns to the central 10°, and the one that renders a bowed line as a bowed line. |\n| C Visual field test | Perimetry in this course is the glaucoma instrument — `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` builds Bjerrum, arcuate, nasal step and ring scotomata on it. It samples far outside the fovea and is a coarse tool for a distortion confined to the central few degrees. |\n| D OCT for optic | Printed truncated (see `source`). Whatever noun was intended, an OCT aimed at the optic nerve images the nerve fibre layer, not the fovea, and the pathology here is macular. Neither this deck nor `L21) Optic Nerve` teaches OCT at all — the optic nerve transcription records that even the Amsler grid and perimetric technique go unmentioned there — so OCT cannot be the answer the material is looking for. |\n\nCompare `opmcq-c13-5` and `opmcq-c13-6` (straight objects seen as wavy), `opmcq-c13-22` (the simple test used to monitor AMD), `opqb-t1-41` and `opqb-t14-627` (what the Amsler grid assesses, and what it screens for).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: op-vissym.)*',
    objective:'Map monocular metamorphopsia with reduced central acuity to the macula and select the Amsler grid, the only test the material assigns to the central 10 degrees, over colour vision, perimetry and optic nerve imaging.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.162, Q15 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECT kept as printed: option D is TRUNCATED — the line ends "OCT for optic" with no noun, confirmed at 3x zoom against Q17 option D, which prints the complete "OCT for macula", and Q19 option B, which prints "OCT for optic nerve". The stem uses a straight apostrophe U+0027. Supporting: `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` (macular symptoms; AMD relevance; drusen); `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slides 84 and 123 (metamorphopsia figure; Amsler grid, central 10°); `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` (perimetry as the glaucoma test); `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 8 (colour vision).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-16', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'60 years old diabetic patient for more than 20 years is developing a nuclear cataract in his both eyes, and his visual acuity has decreased to OD 6/18 and OS 6/24. His vision bothers him while he is driving because he cannot read street signs. He went to an optometrist for new glasses but was told he needed cataract surgery. He was referred for cataract surgery in 2 weeks with a surgeon who has not examined his eyes, the patient asks your advice. What do you recommend to help this patient?',
    options:['Proceed with the surgery to help prevent a car accident.','Advise him to defer cataract surgery for at least 6 month','Refer the patient to ophthalmologist to determine the impact of cataract on decreased vision.','Assurance of the patient and advise him to follow up regularly'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'**The question is not whether the cataract is operable; it is whether anybody has established that the cataract is what is costing him his vision.** `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` states the principle in its POINTS TO REMEMBER: "Evaluation of the visual significance of a cataract includes optic nerve and retinal evaluation to detect coexisting disease that could also affect the visual acuity", and its EXAMINATION list for cataract runs history, visual acuity, pupillary reaction, slit lamp, ophthalmoscopy, ultrasonography and ends with "Referral to an ophthalmologist". That last line is option C. `L18) Cataract II` heads its indications slide "Not all cataracts need operating — shared decision-making is key", and `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` uses the same formula for a different blinding disease: "referral to an ophthalmologist is a must".\n\nThe stem stacks the reasons. He has been diabetic **for more than 20 years**, and `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` puts long duration of diabetes at the head of its adverse risk factors — diabetic maculopathy is exactly the coexisting disease that could be producing 6/18 and 6/24 with or without the lens. He has been booked to a surgeon **who has not examined his eyes**, so nobody has performed the retinal and optic nerve evaluation the material demands. And an optometrist, not an ophthalmologist, made the surgical decision.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| A Proceed with the surgery to help prevent a car accident | The elective indication may genuinely be met — `L18) Cataract II` lists VA below the driving standard, typically below 6/12, and both eyes are below it — but the premise is unverified. Operating on an eye nobody has examined skips the evaluation step, and `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` warns that once the lens is densely cataractous the retina and optic nerve cannot be adequately evaluated at all, which makes the assessment more urgent, not less. |\n| B Advise him to defer cataract surgery for at least 6 month | Arbitrary. No waiting period appears anywhere in the material, and delay answers neither the question of cause nor the driving disability. |\n| **C Refer the patient to ophthalmologist to determine the impact of cataract on decreased vision** | ✔ The printed key, and the material\'s printed sequence: evaluate the visual significance of the cataract, exclude coexisting disease, then decide. |\n| D Assurance of the patient and advise him to follow up regularly | He is symptomatic, below the driving standard in both eyes, and has a twenty-year diabetes history. Reassurance withholds both the assessment and the treatment. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: op-cat-surg.)*',
    objective:'Refer a long-standing diabetic with bilateral cataract for ophthalmic evaluation of the cataract visual significance and of coexisting retinal disease before surgery is undertaken.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.163, Q16 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECTS kept exactly as printed: option B reads "6 month" in the singular, confirmed at 3x zoom; option punctuation is INCONSISTENT inside one menu — A and C end with a full stop, B and D do not, and all four are reproduced as printed; the stem carries a comma splice at "examined his eyes, the patient asks" and the word order "in his both eyes". Supporting: `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` (POINTS TO REMEMBER; EXAMINATION list ending in referral); `L18) Cataract II` (Indications for Cataract Surgery, "Not all cataracts need operating"); `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` (adverse risk factors); `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` (referral is a must).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-17', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'53 years old patient complaining of near vison problems. The patient is asthmatic and has some degree of heart block that is controlled medically. His visual acuity is 6/6 in both eyes. While examination you notice nuclear sclerosis in both eyes. IOP is 28 mmHg in his right eye and 30 mmHg in his left eye, the fundus examination revealed large cup/disc ratio in both eyes. You suspect that patient has POAG. What further tests will help you to confirm the diagnosis?',
    options:['Visual field test','Fluoresceine angiography','Amsler grid test','OCT for macula'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:'**The deck defines glaucoma as a triad, the stem supplies two limbs of it, and the question asks for the third.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` prints the definition as "A triad of • Elevated intraocular pressure (IOP) • Optic nerve affection (cupping) • Visual field defects". The stem gives the first limb — IOP 28 mmHg in the right eye and 30 mmHg in the left, both numerals and both units read off the page image at 3x zoom — and the second, a large cup/disc ratio in both eyes, which the same deck quantifies as "enlargement of cup/disc ratio > 0.3". Only the field is missing, so perimetry is what confirms the suspicion. The deck even names what the field test is looking for: paracentral scotomata in Bjerrum\'s area (10 – 20 degrees of central field), arcuate scotomata superior and inferior, nasal step, ring scotoma (double arcuate), constriction to a tubular field, and a temporal island of vision.\n\nThe presenting complaint is a decoy twice over. `L11)` says POAG is "Most commonly → asymptomatic", with early morning headache and frequent change of reading glasses as the less common presentation — near-vision trouble at 53 is presbyopia (`L7,8) Refractive errors` slide 73: a normal ageing process from about 40), and the nuclear sclerosis with 6/6 acuity is incidental, exactly as in `opqb-t27-14`. Neither leads anywhere; the pressures and the discs do.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| **A Visual field test** | ✔ The printed key, and the third limb of the deck\'s own triad. |\n| B Fluoresceine angiography | The retinal **leakage** test of this course: `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` uses FA to show focal leakage, generalised leakage and capillary non-perfusion in the three maculopathies. Glaucoma is an optic neuropathy driven by outflow resistance; angiography adds nothing to it. |\n| C Amsler grid test | The central 10° macular chart (`L1,2)` slide 123). The glaucomatous defects the deck lists begin in Bjerrum\'s area at 10 – 20 degrees and spread peripherally, so the Amsler grid looks in the wrong place, and it images distortion rather than a scotomatous field. |\n| D OCT for macula | Aimed at the fovea, which is normal here — acuity is 6/6 in both eyes, and `L11)` puts diminution of central vision only in end-stage disease. (An OCT of the optic nerve head and nerve fibre layer would be a real structural adjunct in suspected POAG, but that is *not taken from the course material* — the glaucoma deck names no imaging at all — and in any case it is not what this option offers.) |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Complete the glaucoma triad by ordering perimetry in a patient who already has raised IOP and an enlarged cup/disc ratio, and reject macular and angiographic tests that address the wrong structure.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.163, Q17 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECTS kept exactly as printed: "near vison problems" for vision, confirmed at 3x zoom; "While examination you notice", non-idiomatic; option B "Fluoresceine" for fluorescein. Pressures read off the page image at 3x zoom WITH their units: 28 mmHg right, 30 mmHg left. Supporting: `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` (the triad; cup/disc > 0.3; the visual field defect list; asymptomatic presentation); `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` (FA in maculopathy); `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slide 123 (Amsler grid, central 10°); `L7,8) Refractive errors` slide 73 (presbyopia).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-18', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'In the previous Q, what are group of drugs that should be avoided in that patient?',
    options:['PG analogues','B blockers','Alpha adrenergic agonist','Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'**The two comorbidities planted in `opqb-t27-17` exist for this question, and both of them name the same class.** That patient is asthmatic and has some degree of medically controlled heart block. `ophthalmology.pdf p.265` prints the beta-blocker paragraph in two parts, and each part hits one of those diseases. Pulmonary: the side effects of B-adrenergic blockers "include bronchospasm, bronchial asthma, and dyspnoea. These drugs are, therefore, contraindicated in patients with asthma or chronic obstructive pulmonary disease", and the book adds that "Several deaths have been reported secondary to the pulmonary complications of topically administered timolol". Cardiac: "Because of their cardiac side effects (bradycardia, conduction arrhythmias, hypotension and fluid retention), topical B-adrenergic antagonists may precipitate or worsen heart failure and must be used with caution if bradycardia or systemic hypotension would adversely affect the patient." Heart block is a conduction disturbance with bradycardia, so the class is wrong for this patient twice over. `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` places beta-blockers among the agents that decrease aqueous humour production, with carbonic anhydrase inhibitors and alpha agonists.\n\nThe book does name a way round the pulmonary half — "A cardio-selective B1-adrenergic antagonist, betaxolol, was developed to avoid the pulmonary complications of timolol" — but immediately cautions that "pulmonary effects have occasionally been noted". The option asks about the **group**, and the group is the one to avoid; the existence of a cardioselective member does not rescue it, and betaxolol does nothing about the conduction problem.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| A PG analogues | Increase uveoscleral outflow. `ophthalmology.pdf p.266`: "No major systemic toxic effects of these drugs have been reported" — the whole adverse-effect list is ocular (iris darkening in light irides, eyelid skin hyperpigmentation, hypertrichosis, conjunctival hyperaemia, anterior uveitis, cystoid macular oedema, prostaglandin-associated periorbitopathy). Nothing pulmonary, nothing cardiac; this is in fact the class you would reach for here. |\n| **B B blockers** | ✔ The printed key. Contraindicated in asthma by name, and cautioned against in bradycardia and conduction arrhythmias. |\n| C Alpha adrenergic agonist | Brimonidine. `ophthalmology.pdf p.266` calls its systemic side effects "few" — oral dryness, headache, fatigue, drowsiness (it crosses the blood-brain barrier), local allergy — and its one severe warning, hypotension and apnoea, is specified **in infants**. Nothing about airways or conduction in an adult. |\n| D Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor | `ophthalmology.pdf p.267` gives oral CAI paraesthesia of the extremities, anorexia and gastrointestinal upset, headaches, altered taste and smell, metabolic acidosis, hypokalaemia, renal calculi and rare marrow suppression; topical CAI has a much lower systemic incidence, with local irritation and altered taste. Neither list touches asthma or heart block. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Withhold topical beta-blockers from a glaucoma patient with asthma and heart block, and identify prostaglandin analogues, alpha-2 agonists and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors as classes without those contraindications.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.163, Q18 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). Chained to `opqb-t27-17`. ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECTS kept as printed: "what are group of drugs" is missing its article, and option B prints "B blockers" with a bare capital B, no beta symbol and no hyphen. Supporting: `ophthalmology.pdf p.265` (beta-blockers: pulmonary and cardiac side effects, contraindication in asthma or COPD, betaxolol), `p.266` (alpha-2 agonists; prostaglandin analogues, no major systemic toxicity), `p.267` (oral and topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitors) via the cached `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch20-drugs.txt`; `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` (the medical treatment protocol by class).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-19', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
    stem:'65 years old patient complaining of gradual decrease visual acuity in both eyes in the last 3 months, Medical history of diabetes for more than 20 years. Visual acuity is 6/24 in both eyes. Patient is pseudophakic in both eyes with clear media. Fundus examination revealed bilateral diabetic macular edema and mild NPDR. What further tests could help in diagnosis?',
    options:['Visual field test','OCT for optic nerve','Amsler grid test','Fluoresceine angiography'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:'**Fluorescein angiography is the only investigation this course attaches to diabetic maculopathy, and the material defines the three maculopathies by what the angiogram shows.** `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` splits maculopathy into focal, diffuse and ischaemic and gives each of them an angiographic signature: focal — circumscribed retinal thickening with circinate hard exudates, "Focal leakage on FA", focal photocoagulation, good prognosis; diffuse — diffuse retinal thickening with frequent cystoid macular oedema, "Generalized leakage on FA", intravitreal injection, guarded prognosis; ischaemic — a macula that "appears relatively normal" with poor visual acuity, "Capillary non-perfusion on FA", and "Treatment not appropriate". Since the three types diverge in both prognosis and management, and only the angiogram separates them, FA is the test that turns a fundus finding of macular oedema into a decision.\n\nThe stem is constructed to allow it. He is pseudophakic in both eyes **with clear media**, so nothing obstructs a dye study; the retinopathy itself is only mild NPDR, which `L19.1)` characterises by microaneurysms, dot and blot haemorrhages, hard exudates in clumps or rings, and retinal oedema seen as thickening on biomicroscopy — and mild NPDR needs no treatment in its own right. The whole question is therefore what kind of maculopathy is sitting on top of it.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| A Visual field test | The glaucoma instrument of this course (`L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG`, and `opqb-t27-17`). Nothing in the diabetic retinopathy material uses perimetry. |\n| B OCT for optic nerve | The wrong structure: the pathology is at the macula, the disc is not mentioned, and an optic nerve scan images the nerve fibre layer. The diabetic retinopathy lecture names no OCT at all; the only OCT in the ophthalmology decks is `L18) Cataract II` using it for the petaloid pattern of post-operative cystoid macular oedema. (OCT of the **macula** is the modern first-line test for diabetic macular oedema — *not taken from the course material* — but it is not what this option offers, and the deck teaches FA.) |\n| C Amsler grid test | The central 10° chart (`L1,2)` slide 123), used in `opqb-t27-15`. It can register that something is wrong at the macula, but it cannot say whether the leakage is focal, diffuse or ischaemic, and that is the distinction the answer must serve. |\n| **D Fluoresceine angiography** | ✔ The printed key, spelling defect and all. The deck defines all three maculopathies on FA. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Order fluorescein angiography in diabetic macular oedema to classify the maculopathy as focal, diffuse or ischaemic, since the material defines and treats the three types by their angiographic pattern.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.164, Q19 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️ KEY PRINTING DEFECT, RECORDED AND NOT DISPUTED: the Answers block on book p.167 prints this entry as a LOWERCASE "d" — "19.Correct Answer: d" — while all 26 other entries print an uppercase letter; read at 3x zoom on the page image. It is a slip in the CASE of the letter, not a different answer, and `answer` stays at index 3. ⚠️ STEM DEFECTS kept as printed: "gradual decrease visual acuity" missing "in"; a comma splice with a mid-sentence capital at "in the last 3 months, Medical history"; option D "Fluoresceine" for fluorescein. Supporting: `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` (mild NPDR signs; the three maculopathies and their FA findings); `L18) Cataract II` (OCT for cystoid macular oedema); `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` (perimetry).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-20', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
    stem:'In the previous Q, what is the appropriate treatment for this patient?',
    options:['YAG laser capsulotomy','PRP laser','Intravitreal Anti-VEGF','Oral antioxidant supplements'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'**Bilateral diabetic macular oedema on a background of mild NPDR is treated at the macula, and the material prints anti-VEGF for it twice.** `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` gives diffuse diabetic maculopathy — diffuse retinal thickening, frequent cystoid macular oedema, variable impairment of visual acuity, generalised leakage on FA — the treatment "intra vitreal injection", with a guarded prognosis; and its slide on the treatment of clinically significant macular oedema pairs grid treatment with "Intra vitreal Injection of anti VEGEF" (printed so, with the transposed letters). `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` compresses the whole scheme into three lines: NPDR → OBSERVATION; PDR → PRP; MACULAR OEDEMA → FOCAL & GRID LASER, Anti VEGF. This patient sits on the third line.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| A YAG laser capsulotomy | The pseudophakia is the bait. `L18) Cataract II` gives Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy as the definitive outpatient treatment of posterior capsule opacification, which appears months to years after surgery as a gradual return of visual blurring — and a three-month gradual decline in a pseudophakic patient is exactly what that would look like. But the stem states the media are **clear** in both eyes, so there is no capsular opacity to open, and the fundus finding explains the acuity. |\n| B PRP laser | `L19.1)` reserves panretinal photocoagulation for proliferative disease and prints its indications explicitly: NVD greater than 1/3 disc in area, less extensive NVD with haemorrhage, or NVE greater than 1/2 disc in area with haemorrhage. This patient has mild NPDR and no neovascularisation is described; `L35,36)` maps NPDR to observation, and `L19.1)` says of severe NPDR that treatment is "not required but watch for proliferative disease". PRP would also be the wrong target — it treats the ischaemic periphery, not the macula. |\n| **C Intravitreal Anti-VEGF** | ✔ The printed key, and the deck\'s printed treatment for macular oedema in both lectures that mention it. |\n| D Oral antioxidant supplements | `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` assigns dietary supplements of minerals and vitamins, especially Vitamin E, to **dry AMD**, and reserves anti-VEGF injections for wet AMD. Nothing in the material offers an oral supplement for diabetic maculopathy. |\n\n⚠️ The cached text of `L19.1)` prints the CSME thresholds as "within 500 m of centre of fovea" and "one disc diameter (1500 m)" — the micron sign is lost in the extraction, not absent from the slide, so no dimension is quoted here as a measured figure.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Treat diabetic macular oedema with intravitreal anti-VEGF, and exclude YAG capsulotomy in a pseudophakic eye with clear media and panretinal photocoagulation in the absence of proliferative disease.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.164, Q20 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). Chained to `opqb-t27-19`. The staging records no defect in this entry, and none was found on the page image. Supporting: `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` (diffuse maculopathy; treatment of clinically significant macular oedema; indications for PRP; severe NPDR needs no treatment); `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` (management summary NPDR/PDR/macular oedema; dry versus wet AMD treatment); `L18) Cataract II` (posterior capsule opacification and Nd:YAG capsulotomy).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-21', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'45-year-old woman comes to the emergency center because of severe left sided headache and double vision that began the night before, immediately following vigorous exercise. As you examine her, she continues to complain of severe left-sided headache. Her neuro-ophthalmologic examination is normal, except that the left upper eyelid is ptotic; and the left pupil is dilated 3 mm more than the right and responds very poorly to light. Her motility looks grossly normal. What is your diagnosis?',
    options:['Myasthenia gravis','Optic nerve disease','Third nerve palsy','Diabetic neuropathy'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'**A ptotic lid with a pupil 3 mm larger than its fellow and barely reactive to light is a third nerve palsy, and the pupil is the sign that decides which kind.** `L30) Squint II` slide 11 gives the third nerve montage — ptosis, the eye lying **down and out**, adduction and elevation failing, abduction intact — and slide 12 prints the same case as ptosis plus a down-and-out eye plus a **dilated pupil**. Slide 13 then splits the palsy into two captioned columns, *"Pupil involved (Compression)"* against *"Pupil spared (Vascular)"*, and its diagram draws both mechanisms in labelled halves: on the left the **posterior communicating artery**, whose *"aneurysm compresses pupillary fibres"* — the pupillary fibres drawn stippled all round the **outer rim** of the III nerve — giving *"pupillary dilation"*; on the right *"vascular disease of vasa vasorum leading to infarct of central nerve fibres"*, drawn as a nerve whose centre is solid black while the stippled rim survives intact. This woman is the left-hand column: left ptosis, left pupil dilated and responding very poorly to light.\n\nThe accompaniment fits it. The headache is severe, on the same side as the palsy, began abruptly the night before immediately after vigorous exertion, and is still going on while she is being examined. Of every cause a third nerve palsy could have, the deck names exactly two, and the compressive aneurysm is one of them.\n\n⚠️ **A divergence, recorded and not corrected:** the stem says *"Her motility looks grossly normal"*, whereas slides 11 and 12 show a frankly down-and-out eye with failed adduction and elevation. A pupil-involving third nerve palsy can declare itself through the lid and the pupil before the ductions are obviously limited — *not taken from the course material*; the deck prints only the complete palsy. The key does not rest on motility. It rests on two structures the third nerve supplies: the levator of the upper lid, and through its parasympathetic fibres the pupillary sphincter (*the anatomy is not taken from the course material* — the deck names no muscle for any nerve palsy). ⚠️ The deck also offers **no investigation** for a nerve palsy: slide 13 names an aneurysm and no slide anywhere mentions CT, MRI or angiography, so nothing is quoted here as this course\'s work-up.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| A Myasthenia gravis | Genuinely tempting: slide 18 shows bilateral ptosis worsening on sustained upgaze, and slide 20 prints **"Any pattern"** in red — myasthenia can imitate any nerve or muscle palsy. But slide 17 puts the lesion at the **neuromuscular junction** of skeletal muscle, with antibodies capping the acetylcholine receptors, and the pupil is not driven by one, so myasthenia does not involve the pupil (*not taken from the course material* — the deck never states pupil sparing). Its ptosis is fatigable and reverses with the ice pack and Tensilon tests (slide 19); it does not arrive abruptly with a severe unilateral headache. |\n| B Optic nerve disease | An optic neuropathy costs acuity and colour vision and shows a relative afferent pupillary defect (`L21) Optic Nerve` slide 8, the papilloedema-versus-papillitis table). It does not drop the lid, and an afferent defect leaves the two pupils the same size in ambient light (*not taken from the course material*). The stem states the neuro-ophthalmologic examination is otherwise normal. |\n| **C Third nerve palsy** | ✔ The printed key — ptosis plus a dilated, poorly reactive pupil is the compressive half of slide 13. |\n| D Diabetic neuropathy | Slide 13\'s **other** column, defined by the opposite finding: the vasa vasorum infarct takes the **centre** of the nerve and spares the rim where the pupillary fibres run, so the pupil is spared. A 3 mm anisocoria that barely reacts rules it out. The stem also gives her no diabetes — it gives her exertion and headache. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Read a ptotic lid with a dilated, poorly reactive pupil as a pupil-involving third nerve palsy, and separate the compressive form from the pupil-sparing vascular one.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.164, Q21 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️⚠️ STEM TAKEN FROM THE PAGE IMAGE, NOT FROM THE STAGING: `content\\ophtho\\qb-pages\\gg-tutorial.array.js` carries an entirely different question in the `stem` field of its entries 21-27, while its `opts` and `key` are correct throughout; the printed stem above was read off the page image and matched word for word by three independent readers. Supporting: `L30) Squint II` slides 11-13 (third nerve palsy; pupil involved versus pupil spared; the posterior communicating artery aneurysm and the vasa vasorum infarct), slides 17-20 (myasthenia gravis, neuromuscular junction, ice pack and Tensilon tests, "Any pattern"), and the deck\'s not-covered register items 7-8 (no cause named for any nerve palsy but those two; no investigation offered for any palsy); `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 8.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-22', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'A 64-year-old man presented for evaluation of binocular horizontal diplopia that began 2 weeks earlier and was most bothersome when viewing at a distance. He noted that the diplopia was more prominent when he directed his gaze to the left but otherwise it was present all the time. He had a history of hypertension, diabetes mellitus, and dyslipidemia. On examination, of the patient\'s left eye showed esodeviation and did not fully abduct. The fundus examination was normal. What is your diagnosis?',
    options:['Myasthenia gravis','6th nerve palsy','3rd nerve palsy','Increase ICP'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'**An eye that sits turned in and will not fully turn out is the deck\'s sixth nerve palsy, sign for sign.** `L30) Squint II` slide 15 gives it in three lines — **esotropia in the primary position**, **full adduction**, **deficient abduction** — and prints the montage of gaze positions to match. This man\'s left eye shows esodeviation and does not fully abduct, and everything else on examination is normal.\n\nThe rest of the history corroborates rather than competes. The diplopia is **horizontal**, which is what a horizontal rectus weakness produces, and **binocular**, meaning it comes from the two eyes being out of alignment rather than from anything inside one eye (*the binocular-versus-monocular distinction is not taken from the course material*; the deck uses the term without defining it). It is worst on **left gaze**, the direction the weak left eye has to abduct into — the field of action of the paralysed muscle. It is worse **at distance**, because an esodeviation is larger for distance fixation than for near (*not taken from the course material*). And it has been constant for two weeks.\n\nHis hypertension, diabetes and dyslipidaemia point at a microvascular cause. ⚠️ Handle that carefully: the deck names **no cause for any nerve palsy** except the two on slide 13, and diabetes, hypertension, trauma, tumour and raised intracranial pressure appear on no slide at all. What the material does print is the mechanism itself — *"vascular disease of vasa vasorum leading to infarct of central nerve fibres"*, drawn there for the pupil-sparing third nerve palsy. Applying that same mechanism to the sixth nerve in a vasculopathic 64-year-old is *not taken from the course material*, and the question can be answered without it: the diagnosis is read off the deviation and the duction, not off the risk factors.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| A Myasthenia gravis | Slide 20 prints **"Any pattern"** in red precisely because myasthenia can imitate any nerve palsy, so it cannot be dismissed on the pattern. It is dismissed on the behaviour: the deck\'s myasthenia is **fatigable and reversible** — ptosis that worsens on sustained upgaze (slide 18) and lifts again after the ice pack or Tensilon (slide 19). This diplopia has been present all the time for two weeks with a fixed esodeviation, and no ptosis or fatigability is described. |\n| **B 6th nerve palsy** | ✔ The printed key — esodeviation in the primary position with deficient abduction and intact adduction, exactly slide 15. |\n| C 3rd nerve palsy | The inverted picture. Slides 11-12 give ptosis, the eye **down and out**, adduction and elevation failing and **abduction intact**; slide 13 adds the pupil. Here the eye is turned **in**, abduction is what fails, and neither lid nor pupil is abnormal. |\n| D Increase ICP | Raised intracranial pressure declares itself at the disc, and the fundus here is normal: `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 4 maps papilloedema to an enlarged blind spot and slide 6 shows the swollen discs, while slide 8\'s table sets papilloedema apart as bilateral with normal acuity. ⚠️ The squint deck never teaches the sixth nerve as a false-localising sign of raised ICP — its own not-covered register says so explicitly — so this option is defeated by the printed normal fundus, not by a taught association. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Diagnose a sixth nerve palsy from esodeviation in the primary position with deficient abduction, and distinguish it from third nerve palsy, myasthenia and papilloedema.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.164, Q22 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️ STAGING PAGE CORRECTION: the staging entry `n:22` records `p:165`; the question is printed on book **p.164**, confirmed by all three readers of the page image. ⚠️⚠️ STEM TAKEN FROM THE PAGE IMAGE, NOT FROM THE STAGING — see `opqb-t27-21` for the fault. ⚠️ STEM DEFECTS kept as printed: "On examination, of the patient\'s left eye showed esodeviation" carries a stray "of" and a number disagreement; option D prints "Increase ICP" for increased intracranial pressure. Supporting: `L30) Squint II` slide 15 (sixth nerve palsy), slides 11-13 (third nerve palsy), slides 18-20 (fatigability, the reversal tests, "Any pattern"), not-covered register item 7 (no cause named for any palsy; the sixth nerve\'s false-localising role absent); `L21) Optic Nerve` slides 4, 6 and 8 (papilloedema).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-23', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'A 34-year-old man presented to his family doctor complaining of some visual distortion, he had noticed in the past week intermittent period of double vision and mild ptosis, and then things went back to normal. He reported some difficulties in lifting things with his arm. He did not report any other neurologic symptoms. Ocular examination was normal except for mild ptosis appear with prolonged upgaze and improve with ice pack. What is the proper management for this case?',
    options:['Assurance and follow up of the patient','CT orbit and lab investigation in form of T3, T4, and TSH','Topical steroid eye drops and follow up in one week.','Make an appointment with ophthalmology and neurology clinic'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:'**Ptosis that appears on prolonged upgaze and lifts again with an ice pack is the deck\'s own bedside proof of myasthenia gravis — and myasthenia is not an eye disease.** `L30) Squint II` slide 18 shows the fatigability directly, a patient whose lids droop further the longer upgaze is sustained; slide 19 prints the two reversal demonstrations as captions on before-and-after photographs, *"Ice Pack Test"* and *"TensilonTest"* (run together as one word, exactly so); slide 20 prints **"Any pattern"** in red, which is why his double vision came and went in no fixed direction. Slide 17 puts the lesion at the **neuromuscular junction**, its diagram showing antibodies capping the acetylcholine receptors so that transmission fails. The eye is where it shows, not where it is.\n\nHis arm settles the referral. *"Some difficulties in lifting things with his arm"* is weakness in a limb, outside anything an ophthalmologist treats, and it means the disease is already not confined to the eyes.\n\nSo does the work-up. Slide 21 lists the investigations, and all three are general-medical: **Chest CT, X ray: enlarged Thymus** (the word printed in red, with two radiographs arrowed at the widened mediastinal border), **Acetyl Choline Receptor Antibodies**, and **EMG**. Not one is an ophthalmic test. ⚠️ And the deck prints **no treatment at all** for myasthenia: it reaches the thymus on the chest film and stops — no pyridostigmine, steroids, immunosuppression, plasma exchange, IVIG or thymectomy is named anywhere in the deck, and no drug name or dose appears in it. Referral into both clinics is therefore precisely where this course\'s pathway ends, which is what the key says.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| A Assurance and follow up of the patient | The bait is that the episodes already resolved on their own — but fluctuation **is** the disease, not its cure, and the limb weakness says it has spread beyond the eyes. Reassurance would also skip every investigation on slide 21. (Untreated generalised myasthenia can progress to respiratory failure — *not taken from the course material*: the deck mentions no crisis and never separates ocular from generalised disease.) |\n| B CT orbit and lab investigation in form of T3, T4, and TSH | The **thyroid eye disease** work-up, aimed at the wrong diagnosis. `L10) orbit` slide 15 gives Graves as IgG directed at the TSH receptor with weight loss, heat intolerance and palpitations, and slide 21 shows the enlarged muscle bellies on imaging — but its lid sign is **retraction** with lid lag on downgaze (slide 17: Dalrymple, Kocher, Von Graefe), together with proptosis and a restrictive myopathy. That is the opposite lid sign to ptosis, and none of it fluctuates or reverses with ice. Note the imaging myasthenia needs is a **chest** film, not an orbital CT. |\n| C Topical steroid eye drops and follow up in one week. | Nothing instilled on the ocular surface reaches a neuromuscular junction; the stem states the ocular examination is otherwise normal, so there is no ocular target for a drop; and the deck prints no drug of any kind for this disease. |\n| **D Make an appointment with ophthalmology and neurology clinic** | ✔ The printed key. The eye findings are ophthalmic, the diagnosis, its investigations and its treatment are neurological, and slide 21 is a neurologist\'s list. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: op-lid.)*',
    objective:'Recognise fatigable ptosis reversed by an ice pack as ocular myasthenia gravis with limb involvement, and refer for the systemic work-up rather than treating it as an ocular or thyroid problem.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.165, Q23 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️⚠️ STEM TAKEN FROM THE PAGE IMAGE, NOT FROM THE STAGING — see `opqb-t27-21` for the fault. ⚠️ STEM DEFECTS kept as printed: comma splices throughout, "intermittent period" for periods, and "mild ptosis appear with prolonged upgaze and improve with ice pack" for appears/improves. Supporting: `L30) Squint II` slides 17-21 (neuromuscular junction; fatigable ptosis on sustained upgaze; Ice Pack and TensilonTest; "Any pattern"; investigations — chest CT and X ray for an enlarged thymus, acetylcholine receptor antibodies, EMG) and the deck\'s not-covered register items 20-21 (no treatment slide for myasthenia; no drug name or dose in the whole deck); `L10) orbit` slides 15, 17-19 and 21 (thyroid eye disease, its lid signs and its imaging).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-24', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:'45 years old woman presented to her family doctor complaining of diplopia. On examination there was retraction of upper eyelid of right eye, proptosis and limited ocular movement especially in up-gaze, with mild pain. Pupil of the right eye was slightly dilated and showed sluggish reaction to light compared to her left eye. What is your diagnosis?',
    options:['Myasthenia gravis','Thyroid eye disease','3rd nerve palsy due to microvascular ischemic lesion','3rd nerve palsy due to aneurysm of the posterior communicating artery'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'**Lid retraction with proptosis and a restricted duction is thyroid eye disease, and the sluggish pupil is its optic neuropathy — not a third nerve.** `L10) orbit` builds the clinical picture of TED as five consecutive headings, and this woman carries four of them. Slide 17, lid retraction, prints four figure panels and three eponyms: mild retraction; moderate bilateral asymmetrical retraction, **Dalrymple sign**; severe bilateral retraction, **Kocher sign**; and right lid lag on downgaze, **von Graefe sign**. Slide 18 is proptosis, figures only. Slide 19 is restrictive myopathy — *"Double vision and often discomfort in some p[ositions] of gaze"*, the sentence cut off by the slide edge, with a nine-positions-of-gaze figure. Slide 20 is optic neuropathy: impairment of central vision, to be tested by **visual acuity, colour desaturation, relative afferent pupillary defect and visual field defects** regularly. Retraction of the upper lid, proptosis, limited movement with mild discomfort, and a right pupil reacting sluggishly against the left is that list, in one patient. Slide 15 adds that Graves disease is caused by IgG antibodies to the TSH receptors and is more common in females; she is a 45-year-old woman.\n\n⚠️ **Two details the stem asserts that the deck does not print, recorded and not corrected.** First, the limitation is *"especially in up-gaze"*: slide 19 names no muscle and no direction, and its text stops mid-word. Elevation is the classically restricted movement, because the fibrotic inferior rectus tethers the globe — *not taken from the course material*, although this bank asks the point directly at `opqb-t21-4`. Second, the pupil is called *"slightly dilated"* with a sluggish reaction: a relative afferent pupillary defect, which is exactly what slide 20 tells you to look for in dysthyroid optic neuropathy, does **not** make a pupil larger than its fellow (*not taken from the course material*). Neither looseness touches the key — every other sign in the stem is TED, and none of them belongs to a third nerve palsy.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| A Myasthenia gravis | `L30) Squint II` slide 20 prints **"Any pattern"** in red, so a motility disturbance on its own never excludes it — but its lid sign is fatigable **ptosis** (slide 18), the opposite of a retracted lid; it produces no proptosis; and nothing here fluctuates or is reversed by the ice pack and Tensilon tests of slide 19. |\n| **B Thyroid eye disease** | ✔ The printed key — lid retraction, proptosis, restrictive myopathy and an optic neuropathy sign together, which is `L10) orbit` slides 17 to 20 read in order. |\n| C 3rd nerve palsy due to microvascular ischemic lesion | Slide 13\'s **pupil-spared** column: the *"vascular disease of vasa vasorum leading to infarct of central nerve fibres"* takes the centre of the nerve and leaves the stippled rim of pupillary fibres intact. A pupil sign is precisely what this option cannot produce. Nor does a third nerve palsy retract a lid or push the eye forward — slides 11-12 give ptosis with the eye down and out. |\n| D 3rd nerve palsy due to aneurysm of the posterior communicating artery | Slide 13\'s **pupil-involved** column, so the pupil at least fits — and nothing else does. The aneurysm compresses the pupillary fibres to give frank dilation together with **ptosis**, not a lid pulled up, and no aneurysm proptoses an eye or restricts elevation. It is also the deck\'s abrupt, severely painful emergency (`opqb-t27-21`); this is mild pain on a mixed orbital picture. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: op-systemic.)*',
    objective:'Diagnose thyroid eye disease from lid retraction with proptosis and a restricted duction, and read a sluggish pupil as dysthyroid optic neuropathy rather than a third nerve palsy.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.165, Q24 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️⚠️ STEM TAKEN FROM THE PAGE IMAGE, NOT FROM THE STAGING — see `opqb-t27-21` for the fault. ⚠️ STEM DEFECTS kept as printed: "45 years old woman" and the missing articles in "retraction of upper eyelid of right eye". Supporting: `L10) orbit` slides 15-21 (Graves; soft tissue involvement; the lid signs Dalrymple, Kocher and von Graefe; proptosis; restrictive myopathy; optic neuropathy and its four tests; imaging), and the deck\'s not-covered list (no eponymous lid sign beyond the three on slide 17); `L30) Squint II` slides 11-13 and 18-20. Sibling in this bank: `opqb-t21-4` (the most commonly limited eye movement in thyroid eye disease). ⚠️ CHAPTER SPLIT IN THE EXISTING CORPUS, noted not resolved: thyroid eye disease sits in `op-orbit` at `opqb-t21-4` and in `op-systemic` at `opqb-t19-755`. Filed here under `op-orbit`, because the question is answered from orbital signs.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-25', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'Your neighbor, a 43-year-old woman, is cleaning her swimming pool. While she is pouring some concentrated algaecide into the pool, a large amount of this solution splashes into her right eye. when you hear her screams, you come to her aid less than 30 seconds after the injury Which of the following should you do first?',
    options:['Bundle her into your car and speed off for the nearest emergency center.','Run back to your study to look up the specific antidote for algaecide','Carefully examine her eye for evidence of ocular hyperemia.','Copious irrigation with water from the nearest source instructing her to hold her eyes open to flush out chemical'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:'**A chemical splash is irrigated first — before transport, before examination, before any thought of an antidote.** `L25) Trauma I` slide 9 gives the first aid of a chemical injury as a single instruction with two clauses: it *"Should be STARTED IMMEDIATELY by the health care provider and an ophthalmologist should be called"*, and *"COPIOUS IRRIGATION (with saline 2 L for 15-30 min) is very important to"* restore the pH in the conjunctival sac back to normal as rapidly as possible and to **decrease the duration of contact with the chemical**. Slide 10 adds the second step — eversion of the upper eyelid, so that any retained particulate matter trapped in the conjunctival fornices is identified and removed with a sterile cotton swab — which follows the irrigation rather than replacing it.\n\nEverything in this vignette is built around that second aim. She is reached **less than 30 seconds** after the splash, by someone standing beside a swimming pool, and the deck grades the eventual injury by how much limbal ischaemia and corneal opacity develop: Grade II, corneal haze with iris details still visible and limbal ischaemia under a third; Grade III, obscured iris details, a third to a half; Grade IV, an opaque cornea with iris and pupil obscured and over a half. The three wrong options are all ways of spending the minutes that decide which of those grades she ends up in.\n\n⚠️ The deck specifies **saline**, two litres over fifteen to thirty minutes, while the key specifies water from the nearest source. The material never says to wait for saline, and its own stated purpose — decreasing the duration of contact — is what makes the immediately available fluid right; that last inference is *not taken from the course material* as a printed sentence.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| A Bundle her into your car and speed off for the nearest emergency center. | A drive is contact time with the chemical still in the eye. Slide 9 places irrigation in the hands of whoever is present and immediate, and calls the ophthalmologist **in parallel**, not instead. |\n| B Run back to your study to look up the specific antidote for algaecide | The material names no antidote and no neutralising agent for any chemical anywhere; its entire first aid is dilution and mechanical removal (slides 9-10). The search would cost exactly what the irrigation is trying to save. |\n| C Carefully examine her eye for evidence of ocular hyperemia. | Assessment, not treatment, and it belongs after irrigation has started. Hyperaemia is also the wrong thing to look for: the prognostic sign in this deck is limbal **ischaemia**, a blanched white limbus, so the worst burns are the quiet-looking ones — the point this bank asks directly at `opqb-t25-6`, with the treatment of limbal ischaemia at `opqb-t24-12`. |\n| **D Copious irrigation with water from the nearest source…** | ✔ The printed key, and slide 9 in one line. |\n\n⚠️ Provenance worth recording: this question is printed **inside the lecture itself**, as CLINICAL PROBLEM 1 on `L25) Trauma I`, with the same four options in the same order and **no key printed** there. The bank supplies the key the lecturer withheld.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Begin copious irrigation immediately with whatever fluid is at hand after a chemical splash, before transport, examination or any search for an antidote.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.165, Q25 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️⚠️ STEM TAKEN FROM THE PAGE IMAGE, NOT FROM THE STAGING — see `opqb-t27-21` for the fault. ⚠️ STEM DEFECTS kept as printed: the lower-case "when you hear her screams" after a full stop, and the missing full stop before "Which of the following". Supporting: `L25) Trauma I` slides 7-8 (grading by limbal ischaemia and corneal clarity), slide 9 (first aid: immediate copious irrigation, saline 2 L over 15-30 min, call the ophthalmologist), slide 10 (lid eversion and removal of particulate matter), and the deck\'s own CLINICAL PROBLEM 1, which prints this question with no key. Siblings in this bank: `opqb-t18-716`, `opqb-t24-12`, `opqb-t25-6`.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-26', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'25-year-old man complaining of decreased vision and pain in his right eye after being involved in a fist fight. There are edema and ecchymosis of the eyelids. His visual acuity is OD 6/12 and OS 6/5, pupils are round and reactive, normal IOP and no restriction of ocular motility. On examination of the anterior segment. you notice a diffuse haze in the anterior chamber and early layering of blood Inferiorly Regarding your diagnosis, which of the following would be the most appropriate treatment?',
    options:['Bed rest in 45 degrees and close follow up with an ophthalmologist','Prescribe steroid drops and antiglaucoma drops, and tell the patient to keep his head elevated','Hospitalization and immediately call for an ophthalmologist','Refer to ophthalmologist for surgical evacuation as soon as possible'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:'**Blood layering in the anterior chamber is a traumatic hyphaema, and the deck answers this case by what it says hyphaema does NOT need.** `L25) Trauma I` slide 28 sets out the treatment as a list, and the first line is a restriction: *"Hospitalization: NOT EVERY CASE"*, the exceptions being **children, sickle cell disease, uncontrolled high IOP, and coagulation or bleeding disorders**. Then come **bed rest (45 degree)**, cycloplegics (atropine), topical steroid, antiglaucoma **if increased IOP**, and surgical evacuation for **persistent elevated IOP** or a **total hyphaema of more than 5 days**. This man is 25, his intraocular pressure is normal, no blood disorder is mentioned, and the hyphaema is described as a diffuse haze with **early** layering — day one, not total, not five days. He falls under none of the exceptions and outside both surgical indications, so what is left is the outpatient route: positioned bed rest and close ophthalmic follow-up.\n\nThe rest of the examination is doing quiet work. Round reactive pupils, normal intraocular pressure and unrestricted motility are the findings that rule out the injuries that would outrank a hyphaema — slides 49-50 list a **peaked pupil**, a shallow or deep anterior chamber and bullous subconjunctival haemorrhage as signs of a suspected **open globe**, and slide 48 puts ruling that out before everything else. None of them is present. The acuity drop to 6/12 is accounted for by the haze itself.\n\n⚠️ **Recorded, not disputed: the key is a disposition, not the whole of slide 28.** Cycloplegia with atropine and a topical steroid are genuinely on that slide, so a complete plan would include them; the option simply does not mention them, and no option offers them correctly.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| **A Bed rest in 45 degrees and close follow up with an ophthalmologist** | ✔ The printed key — slide 28\'s *"Bed rest (45 degree)"* in an eye that meets no admission criterion and no surgical indication. |\n| B Prescribe steroid drops and antiglaucoma drops, and tell the patient to keep his head elevated | The near-miss, and the reason to read slide 28 to the end. Topical steroid is on the list and head elevation is the sense of the 45 degrees — but the antiglaucoma is qualified, *"if increased IOP"*, and this IOP is normal. It also has a family doctor prescribing and sending the patient away, with no ophthalmologist attached to the case. |\n| C Hospitalization and immediately call for an ophthalmologist | Directly against the first line of the slide: hospitalisation is **not** every case, and he has none of the four features that would make it his. |\n| D Refer to ophthalmologist for surgical evacuation as soon as possible | Slide 28 gives evacuation two indications, persistent elevated IOP and a total hyphaema beyond 5 days. His pressure is normal and the blood has only begun to layer. (Slide 26 shows where an untreated hyphaema can go — *"Due to decreased oxygen the initially red blood turns very dark"* with impaired aqueous circulation, the eight-ball hyphaema — which is what the follow-up is watching for.) |\n\n⚠️ Provenance: this question is printed **inside the lecture**, as CLINICAL PROBLEM 2 on `L25) Trauma I`, with no key. The bank has reworded two options — the lecture prints B as *"Assurance of patient, and no need for follow up"* and C as *"Hospitalization and giving patient IV mannitol"* — which makes the bank version harder, since its B now contains two genuinely correct elements.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Manage an uncomplicated traumatic hyphaema with bed rest at 45 degrees and close follow-up, and apply slide 28 exclusions for hospitalisation, antiglaucoma therapy and surgical evacuation.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.166, Q26 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️⚠️ STEM TAKEN FROM THE PAGE IMAGE, NOT FROM THE STAGING — see `opqb-t27-21` for the fault. ⚠️ STEM DEFECTS kept as printed: the full stop mid-sentence in "On examination of the anterior segment. you notice", the capitalised "Inferiorly", and the missing full stop before "Regarding your diagnosis". Supporting: `L25) Trauma I` slide 26 (the eight-ball hyphaema), slide 28 (treatment: hospitalisation not every case and its four exceptions; bed rest at 45 degrees; cycloplegics; topical steroid; antiglaucoma if increased IOP; surgical evacuation for persistent elevated IOP or total hyphaema over 5 days), slides 48-50 (ruling out open globe first, and its signs), and the deck\'s own CLINICAL PROBLEM 2, which prints this question with no key. Siblings in this bank: `opqb-t18-730`, `opqb-t18-753`, `opqb-t25-4`.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t27-27', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'28-year-old male patient has pain, tearing and blurred vision in his right eye Earlier in afternoon, while he had been working on his car with a hammer and chisel, he noticed that something flying into his right eye Examination revealed that visual acuity is 6/60 in the right eye. There is conjunctival hyperemia the pupil seems to be peaked and pointing to the 7 o\'clock position of the limbus. There is a small dark, slightly elevated body at the 7 o\'clock position of the limbus, the Left eye is normal. Which of the following would be the appropriate initial management for this situation?',
    options:['Irrigation of the limbal foreign body','Application of a protective shield','Removal of the limbal foreign body using a cotton-tipped applicator','Removal of the limbal foreign body using forceps'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'**Hammering plus a peaked pupil is a suspected open globe, and the deck\'s instruction for a suspected open globe is to shield it and touch nothing else.** `L25) Trauma I` slide 48 makes *"Ruling out open-globe injury"* the first of the three steps of managing ocular trauma, ahead of the focused history and the focused examination. Slides 49-50 then give the grounds for suspicion, and this patient supplies two of them exactly: a **suspicious history — "e.g. HAMMERING"** — and a **PEAKED PUPIL**, the pupil drawn towards the wound. The remaining signs on that slide are a shallow or deep anterior chamber, bullous subconjunctival haemorrhage, and a positive Seidel test showing leaking aqueous. A visual acuity of **6/60** in an eye that was normal this morning says the injury is not superficial.\n\nSlide 51 is then explicit about what to do, and most of it is prohibition: *"Place EYE SHIELD & CALL for OPHTHALMOLOGIST immediately"*; *"DO NOT PLACE A PATCH as pressure can extrude intraocular contents"*; *"DO NOT CHECK EYE PRESSURE"*; *"No further eye exam or eye drops or ointment on an open globe"*; and instruct the patient to withhold food and fluids, preparing for general anaesthesia. The peak of the pupil points at 7 o\'clock and the dark elevated body sits at 7 o\'clock on the limbus: the two findings are the same finding, a full-thickness wound with uveal tissue drawn into it and the fragment lying in it. Anything that lifts that fragment can let the eye follow. Slide 52 adds that an intraocular foreign body suggests globe penetration and is investigated with plain films, CT to locate a metallic fragment and show the integrity of the globe, and never MRI if the fragment may be metallic; slide 45 gives the definitive treatment as suture repair — which is the ophthalmologist\'s job, in theatre, after the shield.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| A Irrigation of the limbal foreign body | Irrigation is the deck\'s **chemical** first aid (slide 9), not its foreign body technique, and a fluid stream directed at a plugged full-thickness wound is manipulation of the wound. Slide 51 forbids any further eye exam, drop or ointment on a suspected open globe. |\n| **B Application of a protective shield** | ✔ The printed key — slide 51, first line, with the ophthalmologist called immediately. Note the deliberate contrast the deck draws: a **shield** rests on the bony orbital rim, while a **patch** presses on the globe and can extrude its contents. |\n| C Removal of the limbal foreign body using a cotton-tipped applicator | The right instrument for the wrong lesion: the cotton swab is what slide 10 uses to lift retained particulate matter from the conjunctival fornices after a chemical splash, and slides 15-16 photograph subtarsal and corneal foreign bodies removed at the slit lamp. Those are surface foreign bodies on an intact eye. Here the pupil is peaked, so the eye is not intact, and removing the plug removes the only thing sealing it. |\n| D Removal of the limbal foreign body using forceps | The same error with a rigid instrument and more force, at the bedside rather than in theatre. Slide 45 sends open-globe injuries — rupture or full-thickness laceration — to **suture repair**. |\n\n⚠️ Provenance: this question is printed **inside the lecture**, as CLINICAL PROBLEM 3 on `L25) Trauma I`, with the same four options in the same order and **no key printed** there.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Recognise hammering with a peaked pupil as a suspected open globe, shield the eye and call the ophthalmologist, and withhold every form of foreign body removal, irrigation or further examination.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.166, Q27 (Grade Gain, "Tutorial Exam"). ⚠️⚠️ STEM TAKEN FROM THE PAGE IMAGE, NOT FROM THE STAGING — see `opqb-t27-21` for the fault, and note that here the difference is clinically decisive: the printed stem gives a visual acuity of **6/60** with a **peaked** pupil, which is what makes the shield correct, whereas the staged text carries a different case. ⚠️ STEM DEFECTS kept as printed: missing full stops before "Earlier in afternoon" and before "Examination revealed", "he noticed that something flying into his right eye", the run-on "conjunctival hyperemia the pupil seems to be peaked", and the capitalised "Left eye". Supporting: `L25) Trauma I` slide 9 (irrigation as chemical first aid), slide 10 and slides 15-16 (surface foreign bodies and the cotton swab), slide 45 (open globe repaired with sutures), slide 48 (rule out open globe first), slides 49-50 (suspicious history including hammering; peaked pupil; Seidel), slide 51 (eye shield, call the ophthalmologist, do not patch, do not check pressure, no further examination or drops, NPO), slide 52 (IOFB imaging), and the deck\'s own CLINICAL PROBLEM 3, which prints this question with no key. Sibling in this bank: `opqb-t18-736`, `opqb-t18-737`.'
  }
